{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And he shall dwell there until the death of the High Priest.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted that Scripture made the atonement of the unintentional killer depend upon the death of the High Priest, because he atones for all Israel and it was incumbent upon him to seek mercy for the people of his generation yet he did not seek it, and behold — the sin of murder occurred in his days. Furthermore, the death of the High Priest is a comfort to the relative of the murdered one, so that his heart will not burn further nor will he grieve over it so much. And furthermore they interpreted: since the High Priest is the cause for the resting of the Shekhinah among Israel, to lengthen their days, while the murderer is the cause for the removal of the Shekhinah from Israel and the shortening of their days — it is not fitting that he stand before the High Priest." "<b>Who anointed him.</b> 'The anointer' — and many are like this, missing the conjugative vowel. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted in tractate Makkot: 'אשר משח אוטו' — this teaches that if before his verdict was completed the High Priest died and another was appointed in his place, and after that his verdict was completed, he returns with the death of the second, as it says 'אשר משח אותו' — now was it he who anointed him as High Priest, or did the High Priest anoint him? Rather, it is to include the one anointed in his days, that through his death he restores him."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:38:00.277016279Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}